{"reference":"Ecclesiastes 6","book":"Ecclesiastes","chapter":6,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Achievement, Death, and “Vanity”","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter presses the problem that abundance, long life, children, and wisdom do not guarantee that a person can enjoy what they have or control what follows. Death is presented as the common end that interrupts achievement and remembrance, while time and chance can break the expected connection between skill and outcome. The repeated word often translated “vanity” is the Hebrew image of vapor or breath. Its force is not limited to one definition: it can suggest transience, frustration, enigma, insubstantiality, or an absurd mismatch between what people seek and what life yields. This helps explain why the chapter closes with questions rather than a neat resolution about what is truly good for a person.","verse_refs":["Ecclesiastes 6:1-12","Ecclesiastes 6:12","Ecclesiastes 6:6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ecclesiastes_006_db9ea26fc7f5d5a13814afa5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}